#!/bin/bash
# commit-msg — a doctrine edit lands on a card, never as a tidy (plan 18, item 5).
#
# docs/HOW-WE-WORK.md is the one text every project on every machine reads at its next session
# start, and docs/INTENT.md is the fixed point it serves. A change to either is intent-bearing by
# definition, so it travels as a suggestion marked "Fix: his", becomes a card, and the card's close
# makes the edit. This hook refuses the other route: an edit to either file in a commit whose
# message names no card.
#
# A card is named by "#12" or "card 12" anywhere in the message. Staging a plan is not enough, unlike
# the machine repo's hook for home/: a plan under docs/plans/ is a card the board makes, but a plan
# that edits the constitution in the same commit is the tidy this rule exists to stop — the plan
# lands first, gets a number, and the edit names it.
#
# Armed by `needle hook install <repo>`, which sets core.hooksPath to this directory by ABSOLUTE
# path: a relative one resolves against each worktree's own root, so every lane under
# .claude/worktrees/ silently loses the hook — which is precisely where a doctrine edit would be
# made. `tests/ratchets/test_a_doctrine_edit_lands_on_a_card.py` reads the bypass (--no-verify, or a
# clone where core.hooksPath was never set), so an unarmed hook is a finding rather than a silence.
#
# Rejected: a pre-commit hook. It has no message to read, so it could refuse a doctrine edit but
# never accept one that names its card, and every legitimate close would need --no-verify.
msg=$1
doctrine='^docs/(HOW-WE-WORK|INTENT)\.md$'
staged=$(git diff --cached --name-only)
grep -qE "$doctrine" <<<"$staged" || exit 0
grep -qiE '(#|\bcard )[0-9]+\b' "$msg" && exit 0
cat >&2 <<'MSG'
refused: this commit edits the one text (docs/HOW-WE-WORK.md or docs/INTENT.md) and no card knows
about it. A doctrine edit is intent-bearing: it belongs to a card the owner ruled on, so that every
project on every machine can read why the rule changed.
  File the learning as a suggestion under docs/slice-suggestions/ with "**Fix:** his", let it become
  a card, and make the edit on that card — naming it in the message: "#12" or "card 12".
MSG
exit 1
